{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (automated cobbler-scaffold pipeline, task #5156) requests implementation of the `tee` Unix utility in Go, specifically covering SRD requirements R1.1 through R1.4 (core copy behavior). Two files must be created: `cmd/tee/main.go` (implementation) and `cmd/tee/tee_test.go` (differential tests against the GNU `gtee` reference binary). The task is scoped to core stdin-to-stdout copy, file output creation/truncation, and handling of \"-\" as a stdout reference. This is part of a larger specification-driven code generation pipeline for reimplementing GNU coreutils in Go.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Differential testing pattern: Run Go binary and GNU reference binary (`gtee`) with identical inputs, compare stdout/stderr/exit code\n   - `pkg/testutils.DiffTest` struct with fields: Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, ExpectedFiles\n   - `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)` runs all test cases as subtests\n   - `pkg/testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")` compiles the cmd package and returns binary path\n   - `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` must be called at start of main() for SIGPIPE handling\n   - LC_ALL=C is automatically applied by the testutils `buildEnv` function\n   - `ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte` allows checking output file content after differential tests\n   - SPDX copyright header required on every Go file\n   - E8 constitution: No function may exceed 40 lines\n   - E9 constitution: Files should stay under 500 lines\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd017-tee.yaml` (READ - full SRD)\n     - Defines the complete tee specification with R1 (core copy), R2 (append/SIGINT), R3 (error handling), R4 (testing)\n     - R1.1: Read all stdin bytes, write to stdout and each named file simultaneously\n     - R1.2: No file args = passthrough mode (stdin to stdout only)\n     - R1.3: Create files that don't exist; truncate existing files unless -a is given\n     - R1.4: \"-\" as file arg = additional stdout reference, data written once (not duplicated)\n     - R1.5: Write in order received (buffering OK but no reorder)\n     - Non-goals: --output-error modes, -p flag, output buffering tuning, special FIFO semantics\n   \n   - `pkg/testutils/build.go` (READ - BuildBinary implementation)\n     - `BuildBinary(t *testing.T, dir string) string` - compiles Go package, returns binary path\n     - Uses `t.TempDir()` for output, calls `go build -o binPath dir`\n     ```go\n     func BuildBinary(t *testing.T, dir string) string {\n         t.Helper()\n         name := resolvePackageName(dir)\n         tmpDir := t.TempDir()\n         binPath := filepath.Join(tmpDir, name)\n         cmd := exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", \"-o\", binPath, dir)\n         output, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()\n         if err != nil {\n             t.Fatalf(\"BuildBinary: go build %s failed: %v\\n%s\", dir, err, output)\n         }\n         return binPath\n     }\n     ```\n\n   - `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (READ - full differential test harness)\n     - DiffTest struct definition with all fields\n     - RunDiffTests iterates test cases, runs both binaries in same WorkDir with same env\n     - `buildEnv` automatically sets LC_ALL=C and merges test env vars\n     - `checkExpectedFiles` verifies file content after test execution\n     - `runBinary` uses 10-second timeout via context\n     ```go\n     type DiffTest struct {\n         Name          string\n         Args          []string\n         Stdin         []byte\n         Env           []string\n         WorkDir       string\n         ExitCode      int\n         Normalize     []NormalizeFunc\n         ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n     }\n     ```\n     - Important: Both binaries run in the SAME WorkDir (a t.TempDir()), so file outputs from both can be compared. However, since both binaries write to the same files, the test harness runs them sequentially - the second binary overwrites the first's files. `ExpectedFiles` checks files AFTER both runs.\n     - Actually looking more carefully: `runSubtest` creates ONE workDir and runs BOTH binaries in it. The second run would overwrite files from the first. This means for tee tests where files are created, we need to be careful - the ExpectedFiles check happens after BOTH binaries run, so it checks the LAST binary's output files. For tee tests, we'll need to verify stdout matching (handled by compareOutputs) and may need special handling for file content verification.\n\n   - `cmd/tee/main.go` (TO CREATE)\n     - tee implementation with sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler(), flag parsing, stdin copy to stdout + files\n   \n   - `cmd/tee/tee_test.go` (TO CREATE)  \n     - Differential tests using testutils pattern against gtee\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet - no code has been written or compiled.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Identified that R1.3 references -a flag behavior (\"unless -a is given\"), so even though only R1.1-R1.4 are in scope, the -a flag needs to be parsed for R1.3's truncation-by-default behavior to be complete. The full append mode testing would be in a separate task.\n   - Identified potential issue with DiffTest's ExpectedFiles and tee: since both binaries run in the same WorkDir sequentially, file output from the first binary gets overwritten by the second. For tee's file output verification, stdout comparison via compareOutputs is the primary check. ExpectedFiles would only reflect the last binary's output.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: Extremely detailed task specification including role definition, repository file listing, full project context (vision, architecture, specifications, roadmap, analysis, source code, requirement states), execution constitution, go style constitution, task description with requirements/design decisions/acceptance criteria, shared protocols, and package contracts. The task is to implement `cmd/tee` R1.1-R1.4.\n   - Message 2: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" - Request for a detailed summary of the conversation.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Create `cmd/tee/main.go` implementing R1.1-R1.4 (core stdin copy, file output, file creation/truncation, \"-\" as stdout)\n   - Create `cmd/tee/tee_test.go` with differential tests against gtee\n   - Run `go build ./cmd/tee/` to verify compilation (AC1)\n   - Run `go vet ./cmd/tee/` to verify no issues (AC3)\n   - Run `go test ./cmd/tee/ -count=1` to verify tests pass (AC2)\n   - Verify all acceptance criteria AC1-AC5\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the three required files (SRD, build.go, difftest.go) and was about to begin implementing the two source files. No code has been written yet. The implementation plan based on the gathered information:\n\n   For `cmd/tee/main.go`:\n   - Copyright header\n   - Package main, imports (fmt, io, os, flag + pkg/sys)\n   - `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at start of main\n   - Parse -a flag (needed for R1.3 \"unless -a given\")\n   - Remaining args are file paths\n   - Open all output files (create/truncate by default, append if -a)\n   - Handle \"-\" in file args as stdout reference (R1.4) - deduplicate stdout writes\n   - Use io.MultiWriter to write stdin to stdout + all files simultaneously\n   - Exit 0 on success, 1 on any file error\n\n   For `cmd/tee/tee_test.go`:\n   - Copyright header\n   - TestDiff function following shared_protocols pattern\n   - BuildBinary(t, \".\"), LookPath(\"gtee\"), t.Skip if missing\n   - Test cases: passthrough (no files), single file, multiple files, \"-\" as file arg, file creation behavior\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write `cmd/tee/main.go` and `cmd/tee/tee_test.go`, then build and test. The task description states: \"Complete the work described in Requirements and Files to Create/Modify\" with files `cmd/tee/main.go` (create) and `cmd/tee/tee_test.go` (create), implementing srd017-tee R1.1 through R1.4. All three required reference files have been read. The next step is to write both files in one pass (per go_style_constitution's implementation_strategy), then run `go build`, `go vet`, and `go test` to verify acceptance criteria.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5156/5eb0e0c8-4d43-4ad3-a168-5eba2553dcce.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5eb0e0c8-4d43-4ad3-a168-5eba2553dcce","uuid":"e9075267-04d9-42a7-9456-d73b0c7ba6bc","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:11:35.725Z","isSynthetic":true}
